 9 months', 'E': 'Ages 2 months, 6 months, and 12 months'} \nTotal Analysis: The developmental milestones of the three children align with typical age ranges: the first child at 2 months shows foundational motor skills, the second child at 6 months demonstrates more advanced gross motor skills and social interaction, and the third child at 12 months is beginning to walk and understand language. The correct option representing their ages is **E: Ages 2 months, 6 months, and 12 months**. This conclusion is supported by consistent assessments across multiple reports from pediatric, developmental medicine, child health, and preventive medicine experts, all confirming the developmental appropriateness of the children's milestones. \n", "vote_history": [{"Pediatrics": "yes", "Developmental Medicine": "yes", "Child Health": "yes", "Family Medicine": "yes", "Preventive Medicine": "yes", "Developmental Psychology": "yes"}], "revision_history": [], "syn_repo_history": ["Question: Three patients present to the pediatrician for routine well-child visits. The first child\u2019s mother reports that he seems to be breastfeeding well and voids at least eight times per day. His stools are a yellow color, and he does not seem to be straining. In the office, he can lift his head and chest when in the prone position, and he can track an object with his eyes to the midline. His hands remain fisted 50% of the time.\n\nThe second child\u2019s parents report that she is doing well at home and seems to enjoy playing with her older brother. In the office, she can roll from supine to prone and can transfer her rattle from hand to hand. She cannot yet say any words.\n\nThe third child\u2019s father reports that he is starting to explore the world around him. He has begun taking independent steps at home. In the office, he seems to recognize the name of several objects, but he cannot follow the direction to \u201cgrab the ball.\u201d All three children are assessed as developmentally normal.\n\