obes', 'D': 'Extra-axial tumor with dural attachments', 'E': 'Atrophy of the striatum'} \nTotal Analysis: The case of the 39-year-old man indicates a likely diagnosis of Huntington's disease, supported by his psychiatric symptoms, movement disorders, cognitive impairment, and family history of early-onset dementia. The expected MRI findings for this condition are atrophy of the striatum, particularly the caudate nuclei, which is a hallmark of the disease. Other options such as reduced hippocampal volume, multiple cortical and subcortical infarctions, and degeneration of the frontal and temporal lobes are not relevant to Huntington's disease and can be excluded based on the clinical presentation. The synthesis of information from neurology, psychiatry, genetics, and radiology domains confirms the diagnosis and expected imaging findings, emphasizing the importance of a comprehensive approach in understanding neurodegenerative disorders. \n", "vote_history": [{"Neurology": "yes", "Psychiatry": "yes", "Genetics": "yes", "Radiology": "yes", "Movement Disorders": "yes"}], "revision_history": [], "syn_repo_history": ["Question: A 39-year-old man is brought to the physician by his wife because of personality changes over the past year. He has become increasingly irritable, loud, aggressive, and impulsive. His wife also reports jerky movements of his limbs and trunk for the past few months. His father had dementia in his mid-40s, but the details of his condition are unclear. The patient appears restless. Examination shows irregular movements of the extremities and twitching of the face and tongue. Mental status examination shows impaired memory. This patient's condition is most likely associated with which of the following changes on MRI? \nOptions: {'A': 'Reduced hippocampal volume', 'B': 'Multiple cortical and subcortical infarctions', 'C': 'Degeneration of the frontal and temporal lobes', 'D': 'Extra-axial tumor with dural attachments', 'E': 'Atrophy of the striatum'} \nT