{"question": "An 11-month-old boy is brought to the physician for a well-child examination. He is growing along with the 75th percentile and meeting all milestones. Physical examination shows a poorly rugated scrotum. The palpation of the scrotum shows only 1 testicle. A 2nd testicle is palpated in the inguinal canal. The examination of the penis shows a normal urethral meatus. The remainder of the physical examination shows no abnormalities. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Chorionic gonadotropin therapy", "B": "Exploratory laparoscopy", "C": "Follow-up examination in 6 months", "D": "Orchiectomy", "E": "Orchiopexy"}, "pred_answer": "E", "gold_answer": "E", "question_domains": ["Pediatrics", "Urology", "Endocrinology", "Surgery", "Developmental Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Urology", "Pediatrics"], "question_analyses": {"Pediatrics": "The scenario presented describes an 11-month-old boy with a poorly rugated scrotum and only one testicle palpated in the scrotum, while the second testicle is located in the inguinal canal. This clinical picture is suggestive of **cryptorchidism**, which is the condition where one or both testicles fail to descend into the scrotum. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\n1. **Cryptorchidism**: The presence of one testicle in the scrotum and the other in the inguinal canal indicates that the child has an undescended testicle. This is a common condition in infants and can occur unilaterally or bilaterally. 2. **Physical Examination Findings**: The poorly rugated scrotum is indicative of a testicle that has not descended properly, as the scrotum typically becomes more rugated (wrinkled) when the testicles are present and functioning normally. ### Noteworthy Aspects:\n1. **Timing of Intervention**: Cryptorchidism should ideally be addressed before the child reaches 1 year of age, as the risk of complications, including infertility and testicular cancer, increases with time. The Am